WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
The Trust Services Criteria are the foundation of SOC 2.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.080
They provide the categories auditors use to evaluate whether controls support the

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.040
commitments and system requirements included in the audit scope.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.540
The five criteria are Security, Availability, Processing Integrity, Confidentiality,

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.965
and Privacy.

00:00:15.965 --> 00:00:17.920
Security is included in every SOC 2 engagement,

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.140
while the other criteria are selected based on the organization's services, commitments,

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.120
customer needs, and audit scope.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.760
Security focuses on protecting systems and information from unauthorized access,

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.190
unauthorized disclosure,

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:33.760
and damage that could affect the organization's ability to meet its objectives.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.200
Availability relates to whether systems are available for operation and use as committed

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.200
or agreed.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.640
It often connects to uptime, resilience, capacity management, incident response, backup,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.505
and recovery practices.

00:00:44.505 --> 00:00:48.595
Processing Integrity concerns whether system processing is complete, valid, accurate,

00:00:48.595 --> 00:00:50.080
timely, and authorized.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.355
It is most relevant when customers rely on the organization to process transactions,

00:00:54.355 --> 00:00:57.170
calculations, workflows, or data transformations correctly.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:01.700
Confidentiality focuses on protecting information designated as confidential.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.115
Privacy focuses on personal information and the organization's commitments around

00:01:05.115 --> 00:01:05.960
collection,

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.550
use, retention, disclosure, and disposal.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.495
Controls connect these criteria to real activity.

00:01:10.495 --> 00:01:12.680
A criterion describes what needs to be supported,

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:16.720
while controls describe how the organization manages that objective in practice.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.895
Choosing criteria is therefore a scoping decision.

00:01:19.895 --> 00:01:22.460
Organizations should understand which commitments they make to customers,

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.240
what systems are included, and which criteria best reflect the service being evaluated.
